# Presentation visual QA

For every main-deck figure:

1. Never change statistical values.
2. Open the rendered PNG after modifying the plot.
3. Judge the rendered output, not only the code.

Presentation requirements:

- Figure must be understandable within ~5 seconds.
- Use human-facing labels only.
- Minimum tick/label text approximately 13 pt.
- One primary visual message per figure.
- Avoid legends when direct labels are practical.
- Use neutral colors for supporting elements.
- Highlight only the data relevant to the spoken takeaway.
- Do not encode evidence status using color alone.
- Remove decorative boxes, gridlines, borders, and labels without analytical purpose.
- Do not put slide prose inside the chart.
- Use common axes across figures that are meant to be compared.
- Keep x=0 in the same visual location for related effect plots.
- Use identical canvas dimensions for animation frames.
- Do not accept clipped or overlapping text.
- Do not use raw variable names.
- Do not use fonts below the presentation threshold.
- Do not replace an existing approved figure without visual comparison.

Before replacing a figure:

- generate candidate A/B/C variants in `results/presentation/final_v1/variants/`
- make a contact sheet via `scripts/presentation/build_contact_sheet.py`
- visually inspect all candidates (PNG contact sheet or `presentation_review.html`)
- choose one based on the slide's intended takeaway
- preserve the previous approved figure as the baseline until approval

Workflow commands:

- `make presentation` — regenerate figures and review HTML
- `make presentation-qa` — tests, contact sheet, variants, palette check

Use `chart_only` mode exports for Figma/PowerPoint; `review` mode for HTML inspection.
